{
  "rules": [
    "When the last 3 days show a strong upward spike (e.g., >5% gain) followed by neutral sentiment, the model significantly underestimates the immediate continuation but fails to capture the subsequent mean reversion; correct by adding +2.0 to +4.0 EUR to the forecast for the first 7 days post-spike.",
    "The model exhibits a persistent downward bias after a major price spike (e.g., Example 5) where it predicts a slow decline while the true outcome shows volatility with a sharp drop; apply a correction of -3.0 to -5.0 EUR for days 1-10 following a spike.",
    "In low-sentiment environments (sentiment < 0.20), the model's linear extrapolation ignores mean reversion forces; reduce forecasted values by 5% when the last 5 days show high volatility (>8%) despite flat sentiment.",
    "The model consistently underestimates the magnitude of the initial post-spike rally; if the last day's close is >70 EUR and sentiment is neutral, increase the first 3-day forecast by +4.0 to +6.0 EUR.",
    "Apply a negative correction (-2.5 to -4.0 EUR) for days 1-5 after any single-day gain exceeding 4%, as the model fails to account for short-term momentum in low-sentiment periods."
  ]
}
